# UST.G3FN - H02 Component-20 Hilbert--Burch Source Theorem

## Status

`CLOSED_EXACT_48_FIBER_HILBERT_BURCH_SOURCE_QGAMMA_IDENTIFICATION_OPEN`

This is an exact theorem at all 48 archived split fibers. It replaces the
first nontrivial part of the component-20 kernel graph by one compact
determinantal source. It is not yet a lift to `Q(gamma)`.

## Leading kernel ideal

Let `R=k[x,y,z]`. At each split fiber, the component-20 kernel determines four
minimal homogeneous generators

```text
g0,g1,g2,g3 in R_11.
```

Their coefficient-degree `0` through `8` syzygy dimensions are exactly

```text
0, 1, 4, 9, 16, 25, 36, 49, 65.
```

The degree-1 syzygy and its multiples account for three of the four
degree-2 syzygies, leaving one new degree-2 class. In coefficient degree 8,
their inherited multiples span 64 dimensions inside a 65-dimensional
syzygy space. Thus exactly one further minimal class occurs in degree 8.

The three minimal syzygy degrees are therefore

```text
1, 2, 8,
```

and satisfy `1+2+8=11`.

## Determinantal source

Put the three normalized syzygies into the columns of

```text
S = [L1,Q2,O8],  shape(S)=4 x 3.
```

At every fiber, the four signed maximal minors of `S` reproduce
`(g0,g1,g2,g3)` up to one common nonzero field unit. On the fixed projective
line

```text
[x:y:z]=[s:t:s+2t],
```

the four restricted degree-11 forms have gcd one. Hence the maximal-minor
ideal has grade two and the Hilbert--Burch theorem applies. For
`J=(g0,g1,g2,g3)`, the exact resolution is

```text
0 -> R(-12) + R(-13) + R(-19) -> R(-11)^4 -> J -> 0.
```

This holds independently at six roots for each of eight split primes.

## Compression and promotion boundary

The only new column, `O8`, has 1,080 power-basis slots per prime. This is a
factor-20 reduction from the 21,600 residues in the prior normalized graph
payload. Seven-prime CRT reconstruction followed by the eighth-prime holdout
passes only zeros and one unit among the reconstructible coefficients; no
nontrivial coefficient promotes by blind small-height reconstruction.

That failure is useful: the remaining task is not to collect more graph
samples. It is to derive `L1,Q2,O8` from the selected geometric source over
`Q(gamma)`.

## Relation to UST.G3FK

UST.G3FK has four selected normals and three selected equations. UST.G3FN has
four rows and three syzygy columns. This matching `4 versus 3` shape is a
strong, exact structural clue, but this theorem does not identify the two
matrices. The required bridge must include the Cox shifts and reproduce the
four G3FN minors in the exact G3EX presentation.

The further 15 generators in degree 14 and 11 in degree 15 are not part of
the Hilbert--Burch subideal `J`; their full homological role is determined in
UST.G3FO.

## Rejected shortcuts

Two compact alternatives were tested and fail at an exact split fiber:

1. The intrinsic conic operator `4*d_x*d_z-d_y^2` has a fourth-kernel with
   the right abstract dimensions `100+36`, but its 100-plane differs from
   every archived component-20 kernel.
2. Neither a constant order-8 apolar operator nor one degree-22
   catalecticant source has a nonzero solution for the archived kernel.

These no-go probes rule out the simplest harmonic and single-moment sources;
they do not rule out the selected Koszul/mapping-cone construction.

